{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by his son Raymond III who was still a minor at the time.\n\n*Early Life and Regency* \nRaymond III was only 12 years old when his father died and his mother Hodierna acted as regent for a short period before handing over the regency to Amalric Count of Jaffa and Ascalon the future King Amalric I of Jerusalem. Raymond was taken to the court of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem where he was raised and educated. In 1159 Raymond came of age and took control of the County of Tripoli.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the Haute Cour the high court of the kingdom and participated in several important decisions. In 1164 Raymond was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1174 after spending 10 years in captivity.\n\n*Prince of Galilee and Regent of Jerusalem* \nAfter his release Raymond married Eschiva of Bures the Princess of Galilee and became the Prince of Galilee. He also became regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond acted as regent on several occasions and played a crucial role in the kingdom's politics.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death* \nIn 1187 Raymond III played a key role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin. He advised against engaging the Ayyubid army led by Saladin but his advice was ignored. The Crusader army was defeated at Hattin and many of its leaders including King Guy of Lusignan were captured. Raymond III died soon after the battle probably due to illness or injury. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III's death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. The losses suffered at Hattin had a significant impact on the Crusader states and paved the way for the eventual loss of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.018178174272179604}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00020382710499688983}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0016012478154152632}, {"token": " nob", "logprob": -0.25561580061912537}, {"token": "leman", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.02954106591641903}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.6682023406028748}, {"token": " leader", "logprob": -0.4662937521934509}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0019388464279472828}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.48157888650894165}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.030370572581887245}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.06200548633933067}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " state", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.20324812829494476}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.001039560534991324}, {"token": " Holy", "logprob": -0.07940739393234253}, {"token": " Land", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.005531837232410908}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.0006282739923335612}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0005677518784068525}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -0.030175579711794853}, {"token": " around", "logprob": -0.693152666091919}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "114", "logp